WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.818
Picture a café chain with a loyalty scheme.

2
00:00:05.818 --> 00:00:10.820
Silver members get 2% of each purchase back, and gold members get 5%.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.309
Mira is upgraded to gold at 10:00.

4
00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:21.253
She bought a coffee at 09:30, but that payment was held up, and reached the program that works out cashback at 10:01.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.889
The program looks up Mira's tier, finds gold, and gives her 5%.

6
00:00:25.889 --> 00:00:32.516
Next week, the company runs the same program again over the same payments, to check its sums.

7
00:00:32.516 --> 00:00:34.796
This time Mira's coffee gets 2%.

8
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:37.209
Same data, same code, two answers.

9
00:00:37.209 --> 00:00:41.167
Which one is right, and why did the program change its mind?

10
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:45.466
Adding a detail to each event from a table is called enrichment.

11
00:00:45.466 --> 00:00:49.952
Each order needs its customer's tier, because the tier sets the cashback.

12
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:58.920
The tiers live in a database that another team runs, and every change to it is copied as a record onto a Kafka topic, customer-tiers.

13
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.605
Version 1 asks the database for every order.

14
00:01:02.605 --> 00:01:09.707
At 3,000 orders a second and 5 ms a question, that's 15 questions waiting at every moment.

15
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:17.629
Version 2 keeps a local copy: a table built from customer-tiers, on the program's own disk, as in the last lesson.

16
00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:22.872
It keeps the latest value for each customer, and a lookup takes microseconds.

17
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.292
Now watch c-1, that's Mira.

18
00:01:25.292 --> 00:01:29.513
Her silver record arrives: c-1 is silver from 09:00.

19
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:33.551
Then the upgrade arrives, and the row is overwritten: gold.

20
00:01:33.551 --> 00:01:34.521
Silver is gone.

21
00:01:34.521 --> 00:01:39.439
A tier change sends nothing on, so orders joined earlier keep what they got.

22
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:45.103
Now o-1, the coffee placed at 09:30, finally arrives at 10:01.

23
00:01:45.103 --> 00:01:51.101
The join looks up c-1, finds gold, the only row it has, and pays 5%.

24
00:01:51.101 --> 00:01:51.895
No error.

25
00:01:51.895 --> 00:01:52.865
No warning.

26
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:59.545
Next week, the same topics are read again, and this time the partitions are read in a different order.

27
00:01:59.545 --> 00:02:01.503
o-1 arrives before the upgrade.

28
00:02:01.503 --> 00:02:03.209
What does the join say now?

29
00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:04.220
Pause and guess.

30
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:07.944
Silver.

31
00:02:07.944 --> 00:02:09.808
Same input, two answers.

32
00:02:09.808 --> 00:02:15.633
A join like this isn't deterministic: two runs over the same data disagree.

33
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:22.389
It's a cashier who charges whatever the board says when you reach the front of the queue, not when you picked up the cup.

34
00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:23.862
Back to the café.

35
00:02:23.862 --> 00:02:28.049
The wall board has one line per member, rubbed out and rewritten.

36
00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:35.494
Now picture a logbook instead, with a dated line for every change: Mira, silver from 09:00.

37
00:02:35.494 --> 00:02:37.249
Mira, gold from 10:00.

38
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.133
And look at the time printed on the till receipt: 09:30.

39
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.067
Which line was in force then?

40
00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:43.533
Silver.

41
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:47.133
It doesn't matter when the receipt reaches the office.

42
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:47.975
Map it out.

43
00:02:47.975 --> 00:02:50.164
The wall board is the plain table.

44
00:02:50.164 --> 00:02:55.187
The logbook is a table that keeps every version, with the time it took effect.

45
00:02:55.187 --> 00:02:58.536
And the receipt's time is the order's own timestamp.

46
00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:01.399
Kafka Streams calls this a versioned table.

47
00:03:01.399 --> 00:03:06.235
The join picks the version with the latest time at or before the order's time.

48
00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:09.149
Run it both ways: silver, and silver.

49
00:03:09.533 --> 00:03:11.587
Which answer was right?

50
00:03:11.587 --> 00:03:12.212
Silver.

51
00:03:12.212 --> 00:03:13.820
She paid at 09:30.

52
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.059
Two limits.

53
00:03:15.059 --> 00:03:17.337
First, the logbook isn't endless.

54
00:03:17.337 --> 00:03:18.786
Here it keeps 7 days.

55
00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:26.103
An order older than that can't be answered: an inner join drops it, and a left join joins it with nothing.

56
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.364
Second, versions fix late orders, not late table updates.

57
00:03:30.364 --> 00:03:37.279
Order o-2 is placed at 10:02, but this time the upgrade itself is late, and o-2 gets there first.

58
00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:40.811
The table only knows silver, so o-2 gets silver.

59
00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:46.821
The fix is a grace period on the order side: hold each order 5 minutes, in stream time.

60
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.961
o-2 waits until stream time passes 10:07, the upgrade arrives meanwhile, and o-2 gets gold.

61
00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:57.992
The price is that every order now comes out 5 minutes later.

62
00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:05.021
In Java and Kotlin, the change is the store: a persistent versioned store with 7 days of history.

63
00:04:05.021 --> 00:04:07.148
The join itself stays the same.

64
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:15.543
So the moral of the story: join each event with the table as it was when the event happened, not as it is when the event arrives.

65
00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:23.475
Or print the answer on the receipt: if the till writes the tier into the order when Mira pays, no later join can change it.

66
00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:25.070
One quick check.

67
00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:29.225
Order o-3 was placed at 09:59 and arrives at 11:00.

68
00:04:29.225 --> 00:04:32.239
What does the versioned join give it?

69
00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:33.543
Pause and think.

70
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.466
Silver.

71
00:04:37.466 --> 00:04:42.701
09:59 is before 10:00, so the silver line was in force.
